770
00:29:12,033 --> 00:29:14,067
- [Tom] Hello! (whistling)

771
00:29:14,067 --> 00:29:18,433
Puppies? (whistling)

772
00:29:18,433 --> 00:29:20,800
- Oh no, Patrick Stewart
fell into quicksand.

773
00:29:25,400 --> 00:29:27,667
- [Tom] And action.

774
00:29:27,667 --> 00:29:28,667
- Hi, NASA!

775
00:29:28,667 --> 00:29:29,767
Ha ha ha, oh.

776
00:29:30,667 --> 00:29:31,633
- Well, that's funny.

777
00:29:31,633 --> 00:29:32,867
- What's that?

778
00:29:32,867 --> 00:29:35,467
- This rip was smaller
when we first got here.

779
00:29:35,467 --> 00:29:38,200
With the stress of settling
into the ground, it widened?

780
00:29:38,200 --> 00:29:39,100
- [Tom] Yes.

781
00:29:39,100 --> 00:29:40,333
- How could that be?

782
00:29:40,333 --> 00:29:43,200
The entire hull is made
of magneridium alloy.

783
00:29:43,200 --> 00:29:45,867
I'd like to examine the
body before we bring it out.

784
00:29:45,867 --> 00:29:47,700
- [Mike] I'll need to be nude.

785
00:29:47,700 --> 00:29:49,167
- Watch you don't
cut yourself, doctor.

786
00:29:49,167 --> 00:29:53,433
- [Tom] Be vewy careful
with my hat, uh huh huh uh.

787
00:29:55,067 --> 00:29:57,767
- That's a nice little Quonset
hut spaceship he's got.

788
00:29:59,800 --> 00:30:01,100
- [Mike] Honey, your
mother's worried.

789
00:30:01,100 --> 00:30:03,800
Why aren't you
getting up for school?

790
00:30:03,800 --> 00:30:06,567
- [Tom] Okay, let's see,
righty tighty, lefty loosey.

791
00:30:08,400 --> 00:30:10,867
- [Mike] Did he have a
skull when we sent him up?

792
00:30:12,267 --> 00:30:13,967
- [Tom] Well, at least they
can strip him for parts.

793
00:30:17,900 --> 00:30:18,933
- Dr. Benson, come here quickly.

794
00:30:18,933 --> 00:30:20,200
Bring my bag.

795
00:30:20,200 --> 00:30:22,167
- Dad never asks me
for his bag at home.

796
00:30:24,467 --> 00:30:26,300
- [Mike] Oh man,
she's gettin' it.

797
00:30:28,833 --> 00:30:30,800
- [Tom] Thanks, I
need my tennis shoes.

798
00:30:34,933 --> 00:30:37,967
- Boy this is cool, this is
just like "Alien Autopsy."

799
00:30:37,967 --> 00:30:39,400
- [Mike] Oh yeah,
yeah, that's cool.

800
00:30:39,400 --> 00:30:40,367
- I want you to see this.

801
00:30:40,367 --> 00:30:41,667
- [Mike] Look how dead he is.

802
00:30:45,500 --> 00:30:47,767
- [Tom] Yeah, it's probably
an outpatient thing.

803
00:30:50,767 --> 00:30:52,900
- How long did you
say he's been dead?

804
00:30:52,900 --> 00:30:54,400
- Oh let's see, ever
since I've known him?

805
00:30:54,400 --> 00:30:56,500
- It's been at least three
hours since the crash.

806
00:30:58,167 --> 00:31:00,733
- Over three hours and
no sign of body rigidity.

807
00:31:00,733 --> 00:31:02,867
- [Tom] Well that's nothing new.

808
00:31:02,867 --> 00:31:04,067
- No skin discoloration.

809
00:31:05,267 --> 00:31:06,733
Temperature feels normal.

810
00:31:06,733 --> 00:31:08,433
- [Mike] Well, let's give
him more time to die.

811
00:31:10,400 --> 00:31:11,733
- No dilation whatsoever.

812
00:31:13,633 --> 00:31:16,033
- Cause of death must be
due to an internal rupture.

813
00:31:16,033 --> 00:31:17,633
- He should've worn a cup.

814
00:31:18,767 --> 00:31:20,567
- I've never seen an
internally damaged body

815
00:31:20,567 --> 00:31:22,600
with no sign of rigor
mortis after so long.

816
00:31:22,600 --> 00:31:24,867
- [Mike] And I love it.

817
00:31:24,867 --> 00:31:26,100
- Let's get him back to the lab

818
00:31:26,100 --> 00:31:27,567
so we can continue
the examination.

819
00:31:27,567 --> 00:31:29,067
- [Tom] Is your freezer empty?

820
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:33,400
- [Mike] There's a layer
of fudge on this capsule.

821
00:31:37,900 --> 00:31:38,900
- It's a girl.

822
00:31:40,433 --> 00:31:41,900
- [Tom] Look at this, mealworms.

823
00:31:41,900 --> 00:31:43,967
I could raise these and
make good money at home.

824
00:31:43,967 --> 00:31:44,900
- Help Dr. Wyman.

825
00:31:46,033 --> 00:31:47,800
He wants to take
John back to the lab.

826
00:31:47,800 --> 00:31:49,600
- [Tom] Sphagnum peat moss?!

827
00:31:49,600 --> 00:31:51,233
- Dave.
- That's really gross.

828
00:31:51,233 --> 00:31:52,533
- Get a blanket.

829
00:31:54,067 --> 00:31:56,233
- [Mike] Yep, I'll
drive this back to NASA.

830
00:31:57,567 --> 00:32:00,167
(upbeat music)

831
00:32:04,333 --> 00:32:06,100
- It all started at a 500 watt

832
00:32:06,100 --> 00:32:08,333
radio station in
Fresno, California.

833
00:32:12,833 --> 00:32:14,700
- [Mike] Please, no terrorists.

834
00:32:16,200 --> 00:32:18,267
♪ This area ♪

835
00:32:18,267 --> 00:32:21,667
♪ Restricted area ♪

836
00:32:21,667 --> 00:32:23,533
♪ Duh dum bum bum bah ♪

837
00:32:23,533 --> 00:32:27,967
♪ Trespassers are
shot on sight ♪

838
00:32:27,967 --> 00:32:31,700
♪ By order of the government ♪

839
00:32:33,767 --> 00:32:36,067
- NASA's fruit truck
makes a delivery.

840
00:32:38,133 --> 00:32:39,400
- [Mike] I have to
question whether

841
00:32:39,400 --> 00:32:41,967
this is really a
government operation.

842
00:32:41,967 --> 00:32:44,233
- Well, we had to snap him
in two like a frozen dog

843
00:32:44,233 --> 00:32:46,100
to get him out of the space
capsule, but I think you'll--

844
00:32:46,100 --> 00:32:47,733
- [Tom] Ow.

845
00:32:47,733 --> 00:32:49,200
- [Mike] How's this work?

846
00:32:49,200 --> 00:32:51,833
- Hello Everglade, hello
Everglade, this is Goldenrod.

847
00:32:51,833 --> 00:32:53,067
This is Goldenrod.

848
00:32:53,067 --> 00:32:54,733
How do you receive, over?

849
00:32:54,733 --> 00:32:56,633
- [Mike] I'm putting a
burrito in the microwave,

850
00:32:56,633 --> 00:32:59,100
you guys want anything?
- Are they receiving us?

851
00:32:59,100 --> 00:33:00,667
- That's not the question.

852
00:33:00,667 --> 00:33:02,300
Are we transmitting?

853
00:33:02,300 --> 00:33:03,867
They don't receive anything
from us, they won't try

854
00:33:03,867 --> 00:33:06,633
and reach us until the
frequency check at nine o'clock.

855
00:33:06,633 --> 00:33:08,600
- Hmm, what should
I wear to a funeral?

856
00:33:11,200 --> 00:33:12,333
- [Tom] Anyone want sandwiches?

857
00:33:12,333 --> 00:33:13,433
I'm heading into town.

858
00:33:15,767 --> 00:33:17,367
- [Mike] Huh, he's
a little low on air.

859
00:33:17,367 --> 00:33:18,733
(giggles)

860
00:33:18,733 --> 00:33:21,067
- By all medical standards,
this man is dead.

861
00:33:21,067 --> 00:33:22,567
- So just like before, then.

862
00:33:22,567 --> 00:33:23,967
- No heartbeat, no respiration.

863
00:33:23,967 --> 00:33:25,600
- [Tom] Never seen
a man so dead.

864
00:33:27,533 --> 00:33:29,267
- Yet his tissues
refuse to dissipate.

865
00:33:29,267 --> 00:33:30,767
- [Mike] Well I'm
gonna make 'em.

866
00:33:30,767 --> 00:33:34,100
- Is it possible he'd be in
a kind of catatonic state?

867
00:33:34,100 --> 00:33:35,267
Could he still be alive?

868
00:33:35,267 --> 00:33:36,800
- No, get out.
- That is a form

869
00:33:36,800 --> 00:33:38,333
of hypometabolism.

870
00:33:38,333 --> 00:33:39,167
- That's impossible.

871
00:33:39,167 --> 00:33:40,433
- [Tom] Maybe he's dead.

872
00:33:40,433 --> 00:33:42,433
- His heart is stopped;
there's no circulation.

873
00:33:43,800 --> 00:33:45,833
You know as well as I do
that when this process stops,

874
00:33:45,833 --> 00:33:48,733
the brain starts to
deteriorate almost at once.

875
00:33:48,733 --> 00:33:50,567
Within a matter of
three minutes, it dies.

876
00:33:50,567 --> 00:33:52,400
- [Mike] It's like
he's dead or something.

877
00:33:52,400 --> 00:33:53,967
- Hello Everglade,
hello Everglade.

878
00:33:53,967 --> 00:33:56,233
This is Goldenrod,
this is Goldenrod.

879
00:33:56,233 --> 00:33:57,700
How do you receive, over?

880
00:33:57,700 --> 00:34:00,833
- I'm a photographer, he he he.

881
00:34:00,833 --> 00:34:02,533
- Maybe after this
long a radio silence,

882
00:34:02,533 --> 00:34:04,367
they'll switch over to
an emergency channel.

883
00:34:04,367 --> 00:34:05,467
- [Tom] I mean,
that's what I'd do.

884
00:34:05,467 --> 00:34:07,633
- I've already tried that.

885
00:34:07,633 --> 00:34:08,567
How about the Air Force?

886
00:34:08,567 --> 00:34:09,967
- [Mike] Maybe they're home.

887
00:34:12,200 --> 00:34:13,733
- Hello, Air Force
emergency, this

888
00:34:13,733 --> 00:34:16,733
is special C-Q-D
Goldenrod station.

889
00:34:16,733 --> 00:34:18,400
How do you receive, over?

890
00:34:18,400 --> 00:34:19,867
- Air Force is so stuck up.

891
00:34:23,167 --> 00:34:25,100
(riffer mimicking
radio whistling)

892
00:34:25,100 --> 00:34:27,667
- I couldn't receive a spot
weather report on this thing.

893
00:34:27,667 --> 00:34:29,800
- Are you sure
it's not your set?

894
00:34:29,800 --> 00:34:31,967
- No, I've already
checked everything.

895
00:34:31,967 --> 00:34:33,600
Will you go out and ask Steve
to come in here a minute?

896
00:34:33,600 --> 00:34:35,100
- Sure.
- [Tom] Okay, STEVEEEE!

897
00:34:35,100 --> 00:34:37,133
- [Mike] I could've done that.

898
00:34:37,133 --> 00:34:38,667
- Hello Everglade,
hello Everglade.

899
00:34:38,667 --> 00:34:41,667
This is Goldenrod,
this is Goldenrod.

900
00:34:41,667 --> 00:34:43,333
Do you receive, over?

901
00:34:43,333 --> 00:34:44,567
- [Tom] Well, he's still dead.

902
00:34:44,567 --> 00:34:45,900
- [Julie] Nothing, doctor.

903
00:34:45,900 --> 00:34:47,400
- Hello, handsome.

904
00:34:47,400 --> 00:34:48,800
- [Julie] Doctor!

905
00:34:48,800 --> 00:34:51,800
- [Mike] He's got
senior wenis disease.

906
00:34:51,800 --> 00:34:53,000
- [Tom] Hey look,
jelly comes out.

907
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:55,133
- What is it?

908
00:34:55,133 --> 00:34:56,700
- Not a lesion.

909
00:34:56,700 --> 00:34:57,900
More like something
had been forced through

910
00:34:57,900 --> 00:34:59,233
the tissue at a high pressure.

911
00:34:59,233 --> 00:35:01,467
(riffer growling)

912
00:35:01,467 --> 00:35:03,633
- Have you guys seen my brush?

913
00:35:04,567 --> 00:35:05,800
- Steve.
- [Tom] Yes?

914
00:35:05,800 --> 00:35:06,733
- [Mike] Yes?
- Yes?

915
00:35:06,733 --> 00:35:08,433
- The radio still won't work.

916
00:35:08,433 --> 00:35:09,900
Dave wants to see you.

917
00:35:09,900 --> 00:35:10,733
- Okay.

918
00:35:11,900 --> 00:35:13,033
I bet they're going
nuts at the Cape.

919
00:35:13,033 --> 00:35:14,267
- [Tom] Yeah, they're
partiers, woo.

920
00:35:14,267 --> 00:35:15,600
- Just make it work, Steve.

921
00:35:16,633 --> 00:35:18,033
We're gonna need assistance.

922
00:35:18,033 --> 00:35:20,167
- [Mike] I'll keep
fiddling with the corpse.

923
00:35:22,400 --> 00:35:23,967
- [Tom] I found a Chiclet.

924
00:35:23,967 --> 00:35:26,300
- Hey, she walked up twice.

925
00:35:26,300 --> 00:35:28,000
- [Mike] Hi, have
you been helped?

926
00:35:29,567 --> 00:35:31,300
- [Tom] Hey, c'mere and help
me shake the candy machine.

927
00:35:31,300 --> 00:35:32,833
- What's the trouble?

928
00:35:32,833 --> 00:35:34,800
- I can't transmit across
the room with this thing.

929
00:35:34,800 --> 00:35:36,233
- Hm, gold star, huh?

930
00:35:38,733 --> 00:35:40,600
- Sounds like a
magnetic disturbance.

931
00:35:40,600 --> 00:35:43,267
- Yeah, could be the power
lines fouled up outside.

932
00:35:44,767 --> 00:35:45,700
What's happening in there?

933
00:35:45,700 --> 00:35:46,700
- [Tom] Ah, big spring dance.

934
00:35:46,700 --> 00:35:48,067
- I don't know.

935
00:35:48,067 --> 00:35:50,167
Somehow we've gotta get
through to the Cape.

936
00:35:50,167 --> 00:35:51,433
- Five hours (mumbles)

937
00:35:51,433 --> 00:35:53,167
I bet half the brass in
the state of Florida's

938
00:35:53,167 --> 00:35:54,000
having a heart attack.

939
00:35:54,000 --> 00:35:55,233
- I hope not.

940
00:35:55,233 --> 00:35:56,167
Enough people find
out about this,

941
00:35:56,167 --> 00:35:57,600
we'll have everybody out here.

942
00:35:57,600 --> 00:35:58,433
- [Mike] Where is up here?

943
00:35:58,433 --> 00:35:59,867
- I'll check the tower.

944
00:35:59,867 --> 00:36:01,433
You stay here and keep trying
to get through, will ya?

945
00:36:01,433 --> 00:36:04,433
- All right, taking you up to
news time with the Chucker.

946
00:36:04,433 --> 00:36:06,000
- Look, if you pick up
anything, give me a yell, huh?

947
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:07,800
- All right.
- [Tom] Sure.

948
00:36:07,800 --> 00:36:08,933
Ram it, clown.

949
00:36:11,400 --> 00:36:12,833
- [Mike] He's getting deader.

950
00:36:14,533 --> 00:36:17,000
- Blood pressure 120 over 80.

951
00:36:17,000 --> 00:36:19,133
Normal for a living man.

952
00:36:19,133 --> 00:36:20,700
- That's impossible, doctor,
you must've made a mistake.

953
00:36:20,700 --> 00:36:22,067
- But doctor, I
checked it twice.

954
00:36:22,067 --> 00:36:24,467
- But you're a girl
doctor, doctor.

955
00:36:25,700 --> 00:36:26,733
- This man does have
normal pressure.

956
00:36:26,733 --> 00:36:28,433
- [Mike] It's as if he's dead.

957
00:36:29,767 --> 00:36:32,433
- That's impossible.
- [Tom] It's inconceivable!

958
00:36:32,433 --> 00:36:35,700
- Take a blood sample, I'll
let the radiation go at it.

959
00:36:35,700 --> 00:36:37,967
- [Mike] Hah, ladder
that same evening, ha ha.

960
00:36:37,967 --> 00:36:39,333
- [Crow] Don't.

961
00:36:39,333 --> 00:36:40,900
- [Mike] Well, you do the--
- [Crow] Well, I--

962
00:36:42,100 --> 00:36:45,033
Mr. Fawlty, she's-a
crazy, she hit me!

963
00:36:46,633 --> 00:36:47,633
- [Tom] Blood Beast?

964
00:36:49,833 --> 00:36:52,000
- [Mike] Okay,
there, I fixed it.

965
00:36:53,933 --> 00:36:56,100
- [Tom] Hey you guys,
ladder's loose again.

966
00:36:58,067 --> 00:36:58,900
- Goldenrod to Everglade,
Goldenrod to Everglade,

967
00:37:01,833 --> 00:37:03,033
come in Everglade, over.

968
00:37:05,633 --> 00:37:07,333
- [Crow] Restricted
changing area.

969
00:37:11,200 --> 00:37:12,800
- [Mike] I wanna find
our picnic stuff.

970
00:37:12,800 --> 00:37:14,700
- Goldenrod to Everglade,
come in Everglade.

971
00:37:14,700 --> 00:37:15,500
This is an emergency.

972
00:37:15,500 --> 00:37:16,767
- [Tom] You see?

973
00:37:18,967 --> 00:37:20,800
- [Mike] Everglade
you dick weeds.

974
00:37:23,467 --> 00:37:25,800
- This is a stupid
space program.

975
00:37:25,800 --> 00:37:28,000
I'm gonna go work for my
uncle's space program.

976
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:30,833
(riffer grunting)

977
00:37:32,700 --> 00:37:34,033
- [Mike] The
outhouse is connected

978
00:37:34,033 --> 00:37:35,567
to the main breaker at NASA.

979
00:37:35,567 --> 00:37:37,700
(giggling)

980
00:37:37,700 --> 00:37:39,767
- Now you stay in
there, Mr. Power.

981
00:37:41,533 --> 00:37:42,967
- [Tom] Very interesting.

982
00:37:42,967 --> 00:37:43,900
- Steve?

983
00:37:43,900 --> 00:37:47,200
- [Mike] Not everybody is Steve.

984
00:37:48,867 --> 00:37:50,800
- [Crow] Steve,
Steve, cut it out!

985
00:37:52,233 --> 00:37:54,467
Dah dah dah dow, dah dah
dah dow, pew pew, dah dow!

986
00:37:58,100 --> 00:38:00,100
- [Tom] And the
Steves are there.

987
00:38:00,100 --> 00:38:01,533
(screaming)

988
00:38:01,533 --> 00:38:03,300
- Steve One, you go that
way, Steve Two, come with me.

989
00:38:05,333 --> 00:38:07,533
- [Mike] This has Steve
written all over it.

990
00:38:09,533 --> 00:38:11,067
- [Dr. Wyman] Are you okay?
- [Dave] Yeah.

991
00:38:11,067 --> 00:38:13,700
- Uh-oh, undercupping.
- [Dr. Wyman] What happened?

992
00:38:13,700 --> 00:38:14,600
- Something hit me.

993
00:38:14,600 --> 00:38:16,400
It was big like a bear.

994
00:38:16,400 --> 00:38:17,600
- [Tom] Was it a bear?

995
00:38:17,600 --> 00:38:18,533
- [Dave] It came
out of the darkness,

996
00:38:18,533 --> 00:38:19,000
it was headed for the lab.

997
00:38:20,400 --> 00:38:23,133
I know I put a couple of shots
in it before I went down.

998
00:38:23,133 --> 00:38:25,567
- Wounded animal that
large isn't good.

999
00:38:25,567 --> 00:38:27,067
How well are we armed?

1000
00:38:27,067 --> 00:38:29,333
- Well, besides his pistols,
we've got a couple of rifles.

1001
00:38:29,333 --> 00:38:31,367
- I think it headed toward
the back of the building.

1002
00:38:31,367 --> 00:38:32,200
- Dave.

1003
00:38:32,200 --> 00:38:33,133
- [Crow] Who's Dave?

1004
00:38:33,133 --> 00:38:34,267
- Are you sure you're all right?

1005
00:38:34,267 --> 00:38:35,767
- Yeah, yeah, it
just clipped me.

1006
00:38:35,767 --> 00:38:37,367
(clattering)
- Steve!

1007
00:38:43,033 --> 00:38:45,667
- [Mike] C'mon, we're
late for biology.

1008
00:38:45,667 --> 00:38:47,667
- Dun duh duh dun dun,
duh duh dun, duh dun.

1009
00:38:49,100 --> 00:38:51,267
- [Dr. Wyman] Wait a minute!
- [Tom] I'll get the costumes.

1010
00:38:52,700 --> 00:38:55,033
- You guys be the Yankees
and we'll be the Nazis.

1011
00:38:59,767 --> 00:39:02,833
- [Mike] Mm, neat, did
you get this at Pier 1?

1012
00:39:02,833 --> 00:39:05,433
- [Tom] Now, that and a 10k
really lights up the place.

1013
00:39:07,500 --> 00:39:09,067
(theremin trilling)

1014
00:39:09,067 --> 00:39:10,667
- [Mike] Steve?

1015
00:39:10,667 --> 00:39:11,500
Steve?

1016
00:39:12,867 --> 00:39:15,267
- [Tom] Shh, don't wake up
Dad, he's in a really bad mood.

1017
00:39:16,633 --> 00:39:18,900
- [Dr. Wyman] Get the windows.

1018
00:39:18,900 --> 00:39:20,400
Tell 'em to get
the other lanterns.

1019
00:39:20,400 --> 00:39:22,700
- [Tom] Let's talk fast
for the rest of the movie.

1020
00:39:22,700 --> 00:39:23,833
- [Steve] Did it get to him?

1021
00:39:25,300 --> 00:39:26,700
- [Dr. Wyman] No.

1022
00:39:26,700 --> 00:39:28,400
- What do you think it was?

1023
00:39:28,400 --> 00:39:30,033
- This didn't come off any bear.

1024
00:39:30,033 --> 00:39:31,267
Bring the light
over here, will you?

1025
00:39:31,267 --> 00:39:33,100
- [Tom] Ugh, that's
mine, my back has been

1026
00:39:33,100 --> 00:39:34,333
flaking off like crazy.

1027
00:39:36,167 --> 00:39:37,400
- No fur, no blood.

1028
00:39:37,400 --> 00:39:40,133
- No disco, no foolin' around.

1029
00:39:40,133 --> 00:39:41,400
- Very much like leather.

1030
00:39:42,767 --> 00:39:43,967
- [Mike] Can I go home?

1031
00:39:43,967 --> 00:39:45,400
- Is the power out completely?

1032
00:39:45,400 --> 00:39:46,667
- [Tom] No, just partly.

1033
00:39:46,667 --> 00:39:48,300
Of course it's out completely!
- Yes, it is.

1034
00:39:48,300 --> 00:39:50,033
- You have any idea
what caused it?

1035
00:39:50,033 --> 00:39:52,367
- Could be break in
the cable at the dam or

1036
00:39:52,367 --> 00:39:54,400
a short circuit in
one of the transoms.

1037
00:39:54,400 --> 00:39:55,800
- [Tom] A squirrel.

1038
00:39:55,800 --> 00:39:57,467
- Dave, are you sure the
building was thoroughly checked?

1039
00:39:57,467 --> 00:39:59,533
Are the wiring and
the fuses all right?

1040
00:39:59,533 --> 00:40:00,600
- Oh, I know
everything thoroughly.

1041
00:40:00,600 --> 00:40:02,033
I checked everything.

1042
00:40:02,033 --> 00:40:03,267
Ah, I'll check again.

1043
00:40:03,267 --> 00:40:04,533
- Maybe they shouldn't
have launched

1044
00:40:04,533 --> 00:40:06,067
a rocket while the
dryer was running.

1045
00:40:06,067 --> 00:40:07,767
- [Tom] Yep.

1046
00:40:07,767 --> 00:40:09,667
- Nothing's wrong here.

1047
00:40:09,667 --> 00:40:12,300
Like I said, it's probably
the disturbance up at the dam.

1048
00:40:12,300 --> 00:40:14,500
- Well, Steve needs some
help boarding the window.

1049
00:40:14,500 --> 00:40:15,733
- [Mike] Get Steve on it.

1050
00:40:19,267 --> 00:40:22,167
- [Tom] There, all set in
case the birds attack us.

1051
00:40:23,367 --> 00:40:24,300
- Well, at least
we won't freeze.

1052
00:40:24,300 --> 00:40:25,833
- Unless it gets really cold.

1053
00:40:25,833 --> 00:40:28,800
- A place like this would
be equipped with heating.

1054
00:40:28,800 --> 00:40:30,000
- Up until a few months ago,

1055
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:31,800
this was a radar
monitoring station.

1056
00:40:31,800 --> 00:40:33,233
- [Mike] Before that
it was a carwash.

1057
00:40:33,233 --> 00:40:34,733
- The ultrasensitive
instruments respond better

1058
00:40:34,733 --> 00:40:35,967
to the cooler atmosphere.

1059
00:40:37,067 --> 00:40:37,967
Systolic was 140.

1060
00:40:37,967 --> 00:40:39,467
- [Tom] So just lay off.

1061
00:40:39,467 --> 00:40:41,567
- I'll have a blood sample
for you in just a few minutes.

1062
00:40:41,567 --> 00:40:42,833
- [Mike] Make it two.

1063
00:40:42,833 --> 00:40:44,033
- I think it'll take
an hour to realize

1064
00:40:44,033 --> 00:40:45,133
we're incommunicado
and send aid.

1065
00:40:46,367 --> 00:40:47,733
Security operation
as tight as this,

1066
00:40:47,733 --> 00:40:49,633
may be another 12 hours.

1067
00:40:49,633 --> 00:40:52,233
Could mean we could
be here another day.

1068
00:40:52,233 --> 00:40:54,433
- [Crow] Ouch,
everything's so little.

1069
00:40:56,667 --> 00:40:58,333
Oh, tiny fried eggs.

1070
00:40:58,333 --> 00:41:00,767
- [Tom] Who order
theirs sunny-side up?

1071
00:41:00,767 --> 00:41:02,833
- Doctor, doctor!

1072
00:41:02,833 --> 00:41:04,200
- [Tom] Oh wait, nevermind.

1073
00:41:08,333 --> 00:41:09,733
- I've never seen
anything quite like it.

1074
00:41:09,733 --> 00:41:11,933
- I've never seen
a saltine up close.

1075
00:41:13,100 --> 00:41:14,233
(riffers laughing)
- [Julie] Notice the way

1076
00:41:14,233 --> 00:41:15,467
it's fighting the others.

1077
00:41:16,633 --> 00:41:18,233
- [Tom] My God, I'm pregnant.

1078
00:41:21,433 --> 00:41:24,033
(upbeat music)

1079
00:41:27,333 --> 00:41:28,733
- [Tom] One, two, three, go.

1080
00:41:28,733 --> 00:41:32,067
(laughing and grunting)

1081
00:41:37,300 --> 00:41:38,800
- Ahem.

1082
00:41:38,800 --> 00:41:40,233
H-Hem.

1083
00:41:40,233 --> 00:41:43,633
- Uncle, uncle, uncle!
- [Tom] Uncle, uncle, uncle.

1084
00:41:43,633 --> 00:41:45,333
(nervous laughing)

1085
00:41:45,333 --> 00:41:46,633
- Oh hi, Mrs. Forrester.

1086
00:41:46,633 --> 00:41:49,167
You're looking nice today.

1087
00:41:49,167 --> 00:41:52,333
- Yeah right, look kids,
my Clayton has something

1088
00:41:52,333 --> 00:41:53,500
he wants to say to you.

1089
00:41:53,500 --> 00:41:55,200
Don't you, Clayton?

1090
00:41:55,200 --> 00:41:56,033
- I do?

1091
00:41:56,033 --> 00:41:57,267
- Yes you do young man.

1092
00:41:57,267 --> 00:41:58,900
Now you come right
here front and center

1093
00:41:58,900 --> 00:42:01,600
and you tell your little
friends what you did.

1094
00:42:01,600 --> 00:42:03,567
- Mother, don't talk
to the experiment.

1095
00:42:03,567 --> 00:42:04,500
You'll taint the data.

1096
00:42:04,500 --> 00:42:07,400
- You come here and apologize.

1097
00:42:07,400 --> 00:42:08,467
- Mother, apologize for what?

1098
00:42:08,467 --> 00:42:09,400
What did I do?

1099
00:42:09,400 --> 00:42:10,667
- Don't take that tone with me.

1100
00:42:10,667 --> 00:42:12,233
I think you know.

1101
00:42:12,233 --> 00:42:14,067
The high school incident?

1102
00:42:14,067 --> 00:42:16,700
- Oh, I'm sure I have no idea
what you're talking about.

1103
00:42:16,700 --> 00:42:17,533
- Clayton!

1104
00:42:17,533 --> 00:42:18,367
- Mother!

1105
00:42:18,367 --> 00:42:19,633
- Clay.

1106
00:42:19,633 --> 00:42:22,333
- Oh, was it the time
I ate the bag of wool?

1107
00:42:22,333 --> 00:42:24,200
- No, the bad thing.

1108
00:42:24,200 --> 00:42:27,800
- Oh, hah, I peed in
Bryan Furlong's locker.

1109
00:42:27,800 --> 00:42:28,600
- No.

1110
00:42:30,800 --> 00:42:33,333
- I puked Coco Puffs
and hot chocolate

1111
00:42:33,333 --> 00:42:36,033
into Kevin McConnell's jacket.
- No!

1112
00:42:37,167 --> 00:42:38,633
- Is it poop related?

1113
00:42:38,633 --> 00:42:40,567
- Clayton, you know very well
what it is and I think you

1114
00:42:40,567 --> 00:42:44,133
will feel better if you
just admit it and apologize.

1115
00:42:44,133 --> 00:42:46,000
- Mother, make it stop.

1116
00:42:46,000 --> 00:42:50,833
- Clayton Deborah
Susan Forrester.

1117
00:42:50,833 --> 00:42:53,333
- All right Mom, all
right c'mon, you and me!

1118
00:42:53,333 --> 00:42:54,533
C'mon, we will dance.

1119
00:42:54,533 --> 00:42:55,933
C'mon, you wanna piece of me?

1120
00:42:55,933 --> 00:42:56,833
Huh, huh?

1121
00:42:56,833 --> 00:42:57,667
C'mon.

1122
00:42:57,667 --> 00:42:58,467
Huh.

1123
00:43:00,133 --> 00:43:02,600
(gun clicking)

1124
00:43:03,467 --> 00:43:04,433
(cartoonish gun shot)

1125
00:43:04,433 --> 00:43:05,267
Ah, oh!

1126
00:43:08,867 --> 00:43:09,800
Ahhhh, gee!

1127
00:43:11,433 --> 00:43:12,833
(cartoonish gun shot)

1128
00:43:12,833 --> 00:43:14,567
Oh, nice shooting Mother!

1129
00:43:14,567 --> 00:43:15,733
(cartoonish gun shot)

1130
00:43:15,733 --> 00:43:17,933
Oh, Mother, blood.

1131
00:43:19,333 --> 00:43:23,433
- Aren't you hooligans
due back into the theater?

1132
00:43:23,433 --> 00:43:26,433
(panicked shouting)

1133
00:43:28,867 --> 00:43:32,533
(whizzing open in sequence)

1134
00:43:39,833 --> 00:43:42,000
- Seven hours and his
blood is still alive.

1135
00:43:44,600 --> 00:43:45,633
- [Crow] Steve, you can still

1136
00:43:45,633 --> 00:43:48,000
lead a full and productive life.

1137
00:43:48,000 --> 00:43:50,833
- [Tom] Just reminded of when
my turtle died, that's all.

1138
00:43:52,333 --> 00:43:55,067
- [Mike] (sighs) What I'm about
to say might sound strange

1139
00:43:55,067 --> 00:43:57,233
but I think we should
eat this corpse.

1140
00:43:57,233 --> 00:43:58,500
(chuckling)

1141
00:43:58,500 --> 00:43:59,700
- One of us will have to drive

1142
00:43:59,700 --> 00:44:01,600
to the nearest telephone
and call the Cape.

1143
00:44:01,600 --> 00:44:02,667
Every minute counts.

1144
00:44:02,667 --> 00:44:04,067
- What is it?

1145
00:44:04,067 --> 00:44:05,533
- [Tom] There's a cartoon
in the microscope.

1146
00:44:05,533 --> 00:44:08,133
- I've seen amoebic dominance
of a cell structure before

1147
00:44:08,133 --> 00:44:10,633
but this is completely
out of proportions.

1148
00:44:10,633 --> 00:44:11,467
- What are they?

1149
00:44:11,467 --> 00:44:12,733
What do they mean?

1150
00:44:12,733 --> 00:44:15,333
- Well, normal blood
has two basic cells.

1151
00:44:15,333 --> 00:44:17,633
- [Mike] Duh.
- The red carry oxygen

1152
00:44:17,633 --> 00:44:19,633
and the white fight infection.

1153
00:44:19,633 --> 00:44:21,667
But this blood has three.

1154
00:44:21,667 --> 00:44:23,333
The third cell, that big one.

1155
00:44:23,333 --> 00:44:25,200
- [Mike] Remember that one,
the big one, he had the hair.

1156
00:44:25,200 --> 00:44:27,967
- Well, that's completely
foreign to any blood structure.

1157
00:44:27,967 --> 00:44:30,200
- [Crow] God I'm in a funk.

1158
00:44:30,200 --> 00:44:32,500
- If that bacteriod is
contagious, then we've

1159
00:44:32,500 --> 00:44:33,500
all been exposed.

1160
00:44:33,500 --> 00:44:34,433
- [Crow] Bacteriod?

1161
00:44:34,433 --> 00:44:35,800
- I doubt if it's communicable.

1162
00:44:35,800 --> 00:44:37,767
- We better get to the
nearest town, fast.

1163
00:44:37,767 --> 00:44:38,967
- [Crow] Okay, bye.

1164
00:44:38,967 --> 00:44:39,900
- I imagine that'll
be Desert City.

1165
00:44:39,900 --> 00:44:41,033
- But that's over 30 miles.

1166
00:44:41,033 --> 00:44:42,300
Isn't there anything closer?

1167
00:44:42,300 --> 00:44:43,700
- [Mike] There's gotta
be a Pizza Hut nearby.

1168
00:44:43,700 --> 00:44:45,133
- There's nothing on the map.

1169
00:44:46,333 --> 00:44:47,800
- [Crow] I'm gonna kill myself.

1170
00:44:47,800 --> 00:44:50,267
- Steve, call the
Cape from Desert City.

1171
00:44:50,267 --> 00:44:53,267
Get in touch with Dr. Zimmerman.

1172
00:44:53,267 --> 00:44:56,200
Don't go into detail;
just tell him to get here

1173
00:44:56,200 --> 00:44:57,467
as quickly as he can.

1174
00:44:57,467 --> 00:44:58,700
- What about equipment?
- [Mike] Yeah, get some

1175
00:44:58,700 --> 00:44:59,933
of that too, I suppose.

1176
00:44:59,933 --> 00:45:01,433
- Well, we'll need
a new power supply.

1177
00:45:01,433 --> 00:45:04,900
A refrigerated truck or an
ambulance to transport the body.

1178
00:45:05,867 --> 00:45:07,033
Oh and--
- [Crow] Get yourself

1179
00:45:07,033 --> 00:45:08,367
something nice.
- Tell 'em to request

1180
00:45:08,367 --> 00:45:09,900
priority clearance
directly to Walter Reed

1181
00:45:09,900 --> 00:45:11,000
Hospital in Washington.

1182
00:45:11,000 --> 00:45:11,933
- Right.

1183
00:45:11,933 --> 00:45:13,400
I'll be back as soon as I can.

1184
00:45:13,400 --> 00:45:15,400
- [Tom] Oh, and snag some
brews and a couple sandwiches

1185
00:45:15,400 --> 00:45:16,967
and a thing of wax worms.

1186
00:45:16,967 --> 00:45:19,000
- Keep checking the
blood pressure, doctor.

1187
00:45:19,000 --> 00:45:21,533
- The last count
was 140 over 80.

1188
00:45:22,633 --> 00:45:24,267
- [Crow] What a
selfish thing to say.

1189
00:45:24,267 --> 00:45:26,000
- That's all we can do is--
- [Crow] I'm gonna

1190
00:45:26,000 --> 00:45:27,867
go walk in front of a bus.

1191
00:45:27,867 --> 00:45:30,133
What's the point of all of this?

1192
00:45:30,133 --> 00:45:32,400
- [Tom] Look at
those little guys go.

1193
00:45:32,400 --> 00:45:33,233
Wow.

1194
00:45:35,567 --> 00:45:37,733
- [Mike] Could you turn
the light off in the hall?

1195
00:45:39,133 --> 00:45:42,100
- Oh, so like a blood beast can
dismantle a distributor cap?

1196
00:45:43,533 --> 00:45:45,333
- [Mike] Time to
dance, blood beast.

1197
00:45:46,733 --> 00:45:49,467
- [Crow] I bet it's those nuns
from "The Sound of Music."

1198
00:45:51,533 --> 00:45:54,600
- [Tom] Steve!

1199
00:45:54,600 --> 00:45:55,867
- [Mike] Steve?

1200
00:45:58,233 --> 00:46:00,333
- Any luck with the Jeep?

1201
00:46:00,333 --> 00:46:01,500
- I can't figure it out.

1202
00:46:02,967 --> 00:46:05,533
The coils in both the truck
and the Jeep are burned out.

1203
00:46:05,533 --> 00:46:06,767
- [Tom] That's the
most insulting thing

1204
00:46:06,767 --> 00:46:07,900
you've ever said to me.

1205
00:46:11,400 --> 00:46:14,200
- [Crow] I suppose
I should apologize.

1206
00:46:14,200 --> 00:46:15,033
Yowww!

1207
00:46:15,033 --> 00:46:16,733
Oh oh, ho ho.

1208
00:46:16,733 --> 00:46:18,833
- [Tom] Anyway, thanks
for listening. I...

1209
00:46:21,133 --> 00:46:24,233
- [Mike] Look, we missed
the first half of "Friends"?

1210
00:46:24,233 --> 00:46:25,500
- They're both stopped.

1211
00:46:25,500 --> 00:46:27,533
- Everything's goin'
wrong in this movie!

1212
00:46:28,933 --> 00:46:30,500
- [Mike] He's an odd little man.

1213
00:46:32,433 --> 00:46:34,667
- [Tom] I just developed
a picture, everyone!

1214
00:46:36,300 --> 00:46:37,233
- [Crow] If you're looking for

1215
00:46:37,233 --> 00:46:38,700
that one thing, Barb moved it.

1216
00:46:40,433 --> 00:46:43,133
- [Mike] Steve, Steve and I
have something to tell you.

1217
00:46:45,300 --> 00:46:47,167
- [Dr. Wyman] Take a look.
- [Mike] This was in my tuna.

1218
00:46:47,167 --> 00:46:48,733
- We're in a
magnetic forcefield.

1219
00:46:48,733 --> 00:46:50,133
- [Julie] A what?

1220
00:46:50,133 --> 00:46:51,467
- A net of static
electricity is surrounding

1221
00:46:51,467 --> 00:46:52,933
the entire area.

1222
00:46:52,933 --> 00:46:54,967
- That's why our power's dead.

1223
00:46:54,967 --> 00:46:57,400
- Well, we're not
going anywhere tonight.

1224
00:46:57,400 --> 00:46:59,933
The truck and the Jeep are
burned out electrically.

1225
00:46:59,933 --> 00:47:01,400
- What would cause
a thing like that?

1226
00:47:01,400 --> 00:47:02,900
- [Tom] God hates us.

1227
00:47:02,900 --> 00:47:05,567
- There's a very
powerful magnetic
source somewhere nearby.

1228
00:47:05,567 --> 00:47:08,167
It's reversing all
positive electrical power.

1229
00:47:08,167 --> 00:47:10,400
- You sly boots.

1230
00:47:10,400 --> 00:47:13,167
- Dr. Wyman, how close is
the satellite compartment?

1231
00:47:13,167 --> 00:47:14,100
- [Crow] Who cares?

1232
00:47:14,100 --> 00:47:15,633
- Two, maybe three miles.

1233
00:47:15,633 --> 00:47:17,633
- [Dave] Just thinking, is it
possible that the compartment

1234
00:47:17,633 --> 00:47:20,400
could be bombarded with
electron particles?

1235
00:47:20,400 --> 00:47:21,367
- Could be, it's
happened before.

1236
00:47:21,367 --> 00:47:22,600
Some of the earlier (mumbles)

1237
00:47:22,600 --> 00:47:23,600
- [Crow] Snarf farm?

1238
00:47:24,800 --> 00:47:26,000
- When the missiles
came back to Earth,

1239
00:47:26,000 --> 00:47:28,100
they were pretty
heavily magnetized.

1240
00:47:28,100 --> 00:47:30,567
- There's a mountain range
between us and the compartment.

1241
00:47:30,567 --> 00:47:31,767
- Yeah, well, that doesn't seem

1242
00:47:31,767 --> 00:47:33,700
to be any logic
behind any of this.

1243
00:47:33,700 --> 00:47:35,800
- Here's something to
complicate things even more.

1244
00:47:35,800 --> 00:47:37,300
- I took these upside down!

1245
00:47:37,300 --> 00:47:39,833
- The shot I took when Dave
and I first got out there.

1246
00:47:39,833 --> 00:47:40,767
Look at it closely.

1247
00:47:40,767 --> 00:47:41,867
- [Mike] Now, lick it.

1248
00:47:43,367 --> 00:47:45,567
- Dave, you remember I
mentioned the strange mud?

1249
00:47:45,567 --> 00:47:46,767
- Yes.

1250
00:47:46,767 --> 00:47:49,200
- Well, this was
the last shot taken.

1251
00:47:49,200 --> 00:47:52,567
Most of the mud has disappeared
and the opening is larger.

1252
00:47:52,567 --> 00:47:53,667
- Yes, I remember
that when we took

1253
00:47:53,667 --> 00:47:55,500
John out of the compartment.

1254
00:47:55,500 --> 00:47:57,500
But at the time it
didn't seem important.

1255
00:47:58,667 --> 00:48:00,767
- Someone or something
must've been there.

1256
00:48:00,767 --> 00:48:02,333
- [Mike] Saw a huge
blood sucking alien

1257
00:48:02,333 --> 00:48:04,800
but I didn't think it
was important either.

1258
00:48:04,800 --> 00:48:09,233
- Ahh, I'm gonna go kill
myself again. I don't...

1259
00:48:09,233 --> 00:48:10,500
- [Tom] Steve?

1260
00:48:10,500 --> 00:48:11,333
Steve!

1261
00:48:12,500 --> 00:48:14,333
- [Mike] Hey, anyone
remember Pringles?

1262
00:48:14,333 --> 00:48:16,333
- There's an explanation and
a good one for all of this.

1263
00:48:16,333 --> 00:48:17,367
- Elves!

1264
00:48:17,367 --> 00:48:18,600
- There must be.

1265
00:48:18,600 --> 00:48:20,367
- [Mike] Hold on, I
guess there's not.

1266
00:48:20,367 --> 00:48:21,400
- Some of the things
that have occurred today

1267
00:48:21,400 --> 00:48:23,000
are without precedent.

1268
00:48:23,000 --> 00:48:24,500
- [Tom] Ah, just ignore me.

1269
00:48:24,500 --> 00:48:26,867
- There's a man in there
alive that should be dead,

1270
00:48:26,867 --> 00:48:28,800
something that's
never happened before.

1271
00:48:28,800 --> 00:48:31,200
- [Mike] Well, once, but he
had the wind at his back.

1272
00:48:31,200 --> 00:48:33,000
- We never sent a man so
far into space before.

1273
00:48:33,000 --> 00:48:34,733
- So little food and no air.

1274
00:48:36,200 --> 00:48:39,667
- Well, we're all
tired, disappointed.

1275
00:48:39,667 --> 00:48:41,000
- Stupid.

1276
00:48:41,000 --> 00:48:42,200
- Under a terrible strain.

1277
00:48:42,200 --> 00:48:43,233
- [Mike] I feel good.

1278
00:48:44,367 --> 00:48:46,033
- After this long,
the Cape has probably

1279
00:48:46,033 --> 00:48:47,367
contacted the Pentagon.

1280
00:48:47,367 --> 00:48:48,567
It's just a matter of time.

1281
00:48:48,567 --> 00:48:49,767
- What if they
can't get through?

1282
00:48:49,767 --> 00:48:52,000
The magnetic force
field works both ways.

1283
00:48:52,000 --> 00:48:53,500
- [Tom] Okay, so I'm wrong.

1284
00:48:53,500 --> 00:48:54,500
- Well, in the morning one
of us will have to hike

1285
00:48:54,500 --> 00:48:56,133
down and meet them.

1286
00:48:56,133 --> 00:48:58,500
As a precautionary measure
I suggest we all stay

1287
00:48:58,500 --> 00:48:59,433
in this room tonight.

1288
00:48:59,433 --> 00:49:00,967
- [Crow] I'll lay on Sharon.

1289
00:49:00,967 --> 00:49:03,000
- Dave and I will take
turns watching the corpse.

1290
00:49:03,000 --> 00:49:04,467
All right.

1291
00:49:04,467 --> 00:49:05,867
- [Tom] Hey, you're taller
than last year, honey.

1292
00:49:07,600 --> 00:49:08,433
- Condescend, condescend.

1293
00:49:08,433 --> 00:49:09,267
- Julie.

1294
00:49:10,200 --> 00:49:11,633
(crying)

1295
00:49:11,633 --> 00:49:13,300
- I'm sorry, doctor.

1296
00:49:14,500 --> 00:49:16,300
I just can't hold
it back any longer.

1297
00:49:16,300 --> 00:49:18,900
- [Mike] I have to overact.

1298
00:49:18,900 --> 00:49:20,400
- I wish there was
something I could say.

1299
00:49:20,400 --> 00:49:22,067
- I guess I just said that.

1300
00:49:22,067 --> 00:49:24,433
- I'll listen if you like.

1301
00:49:24,433 --> 00:49:27,333
♪ Girl, you'll be a woman soon ♪

1302
00:49:27,333 --> 00:49:29,200
- It's bad enough
losing someone you love.

1303
00:49:29,200 --> 00:49:31,933
But this, the unknown,
being held here.

1304
00:49:33,967 --> 00:49:35,500
- [Tom] Yeeeugh!

1305
00:49:35,500 --> 00:49:37,267
- All exiled.

1306
00:49:37,267 --> 00:49:39,867
- [Mike] You know, my
gown opens from the rear.

1307
00:49:41,100 --> 00:49:42,800
(sobbing)

1308
00:49:42,800 --> 00:49:43,967
- [Tom] Say, hello.

1309
00:49:43,967 --> 00:49:44,800
- Julie.

1310
00:49:46,233 --> 00:49:48,733
He alone stood on the
threshold of our future.

1311
00:49:48,733 --> 00:49:50,933
- [Mike] And he peed
it down his leg.

1312
00:49:50,933 --> 00:49:52,133
- From what happened
to him today,

1313
00:49:52,133 --> 00:49:54,733
we may learn more
than we've ever known.

1314
00:49:54,733 --> 00:49:57,767
- We've learned not to send
Pollacks into space, phew.

1315
00:49:59,333 --> 00:50:00,933
- Maybe I should've judged this.

1316
00:50:02,267 --> 00:50:03,233
- [Tom] There'll
be other Steves.

1317
00:50:03,233 --> 00:50:04,500
- He may be gone.

1318
00:50:05,867 --> 00:50:07,633
- [Mike] Oh mamma
that is so sweet.

1319
00:50:10,133 --> 00:50:11,567
- But is he dead?

1320
00:50:12,800 --> 00:50:15,433
- Look, let's not make a
federal case outta this.

1321
00:50:17,200 --> 00:50:18,367
- [Tom] You know, the
listening thing went over

1322
00:50:18,367 --> 00:50:19,500
like a lead balloon.

1323
00:50:22,333 --> 00:50:23,733
- [Dr. Wyman] Scientists,
the more we learn,

1324
00:50:23,733 --> 00:50:25,267
the more we realize
there is to learn.

1325
00:50:25,267 --> 00:50:26,767
- [Mike] Scientists?

1326
00:50:26,767 --> 00:50:28,200
- [Dr. Wyman] Life itself
is the greatest miracle.

1327
00:50:28,200 --> 00:50:31,467
Well, when we get him to
the hospital tomorrow,

1328
00:50:31,467 --> 00:50:33,133
maybe we'll know.

1329
00:50:33,133 --> 00:50:35,300
- [Mike] Ugh, all my adenoids.

1330
00:50:35,300 --> 00:50:37,533
(sniffles)

1331
00:50:39,600 --> 00:50:42,400
- [Crow] Could you
massage my front now?

1332
00:50:42,400 --> 00:50:43,867
- Good night, doctor.

1333
00:50:43,867 --> 00:50:45,100
- Good night, Julie.

1334
00:50:45,100 --> 00:50:46,200
- [Tom] Those three
little ties in the back,

1335
00:50:46,200 --> 00:50:47,067
slips right off.

1336
00:50:49,033 --> 00:50:51,200
- [Mike] Well, I just
needed to talk to somebody.

1337
00:50:51,200 --> 00:50:52,400
I've been a little down.

1338
00:50:52,400 --> 00:50:53,200
Thanks a lot.

1339
00:50:55,200 --> 00:50:56,733
- You need one of these, huh?

1340
00:51:03,933 --> 00:51:06,033
- [Mike] Oh, here
comes the nutty part.

1341
00:51:07,567 --> 00:51:09,367
(farting sound)

1342
00:51:09,367 --> 00:51:11,733
- [Tom] It's an easy one.
- [Mike] Now, come on.

1343
00:51:12,933 --> 00:51:15,233
- 'Twas brillig, and
the slithy toves of,

1344
00:51:15,233 --> 00:51:17,067
oh, what the hell am I thinking?

1345
00:51:18,800 --> 00:51:20,000
- [Mike] Martha Graham!

1346
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:22,233
- [Tom] Who put my
sweater in the dryer?

1347
00:51:22,233 --> 00:51:25,967
(old-fashioned horror music)

1348
00:51:27,300 --> 00:51:29,967
(creaking)

1349
00:51:29,967 --> 00:51:31,700
- [Tom] Sorry!

1350
00:51:31,700 --> 00:51:35,533
- [Mike] Wait, there's one
last piece of carrot cake.

1351
00:51:35,533 --> 00:51:37,433
I'm gonna have it, he he he.

1352
00:51:40,867 --> 00:51:41,800
- Hey!

1353
00:51:41,800 --> 00:51:43,100
- What is it?

1354
00:51:43,100 --> 00:51:44,067
- [Mike] Did you put
the blood beast out?

1355
00:51:44,067 --> 00:51:45,300
- I hear something in there.

1356
00:51:48,200 --> 00:51:51,133
- [Tom] Be vewy quiet, we're
hunting blood beast, he he he.

1357
00:51:52,567 --> 00:51:53,633
- The light's out.

1358
00:51:53,633 --> 00:51:54,467
- [Mike] Loin chop?

1359
00:51:54,467 --> 00:51:55,833
No, I don't think so.

1360
00:51:57,333 --> 00:51:58,900
Huh?

1361
00:51:58,900 --> 00:52:02,367
- Ew, the patient
has blown a gasket.

1362
00:52:04,567 --> 00:52:06,967
- [Tom] Hi, we're looking
for an honest man?

1363
00:52:08,067 --> 00:52:09,167
- Dr. Wyman.

1364
00:52:09,167 --> 00:52:10,500
- [Mike] Put some pants on.

1365
00:52:10,500 --> 00:52:11,867
- Half his head's gone.

1366
00:52:11,867 --> 00:52:13,667
- [Tom] Oh, that's
his hinder, Steve.

1367
00:52:15,067 --> 00:52:17,533
- Yep, it's the dismount
that always impales 'em.

1368
00:52:19,367 --> 00:52:20,833
- Dr. Wyman!

1369
00:52:20,833 --> 00:52:23,067
- [Mike] You march straight
down from there, mister.

1370
00:52:24,300 --> 00:52:25,467
- There's nothing you can do.

1371
00:52:25,467 --> 00:52:27,033
- [Tom] Unless you
know where his face is.

1372
00:52:29,200 --> 00:52:31,233
- [Mike] Another long
scene in here should help.

1373
00:52:31,233 --> 00:52:32,467
(giggling)

1374
00:52:32,467 --> 00:52:34,433
- [Steve] Must've
taken the body.

1375
00:52:34,433 --> 00:52:35,633
- Whatever it is, it works fast.

1376
00:52:35,633 --> 00:52:37,333
- [Crow] To provide
soothing relief.

1377
00:52:37,333 --> 00:52:38,833
- Let's take him down.

1378
00:52:38,833 --> 00:52:40,133
- [Tom] You said he could
stay up until New Years

1379
00:52:40,133 --> 00:52:41,800
and that means
Christmas is over.

1380
00:52:44,667 --> 00:52:47,167
- [Mike] I forgot to tell
him that Rhonda called.

1381
00:52:49,233 --> 00:52:50,500
- [Tom] Oh, oh God, I
slipped on his face.

1382
00:52:50,500 --> 00:52:51,400
- [Crow] Ew!

1383
00:52:51,400 --> 00:52:53,067
- [Mike] Oh, oh God.

1384
00:52:53,067 --> 00:52:54,800
(screaming)

1385
00:52:54,800 --> 00:52:56,567
- [Tom] Yeah, I get
that a lot, buh.

1386
00:52:58,333 --> 00:53:00,633
- Oh, I just blew all
over the operating room.

1387
00:53:05,200 --> 00:53:07,267
- [Mike] Well, welcome
back to the land

1388
00:53:07,267 --> 00:53:08,533
of the living, mister.

1389
00:53:11,433 --> 00:53:12,667
- Steve.

1390
00:53:12,667 --> 00:53:13,667
- [Tom] What, me
Steve or he Steve?

1391
00:53:13,667 --> 00:53:15,200
- Help him to the table.

1392
00:53:15,200 --> 00:53:17,967
- Let's shoot him back into
space and see what happens.

1393
00:53:18,967 --> 00:53:20,633
- [Mike] How 'bout hello, hi?

1394
00:53:20,633 --> 00:53:21,500
Anything?

1395
00:53:23,500 --> 00:53:25,700
- Johnny.
- Oh, Steve's name is Johnny!

1396
00:53:25,700 --> 00:53:28,333
- Johnny, you're all right.

1397
00:53:28,333 --> 00:53:32,033
- Julie, I don't know.

1398
00:53:32,033 --> 00:53:33,367
- [Mike] I dunno.

1399
00:53:33,367 --> 00:53:34,567
I think so.

1400
00:53:34,567 --> 00:53:36,167
- [Tom] Can I have
an Eskimo pie?

1401
00:53:38,100 --> 00:53:39,367
- Where's Dr. Wyman?

1402
00:53:39,367 --> 00:53:40,833
- [Mike] I hadn't
finished his head.

1403
00:53:43,467 --> 00:53:44,933
- What happened?

1404
00:53:44,933 --> 00:53:48,133
- Won't you tell
us what happened?

1405
00:53:48,133 --> 00:53:50,533
- Julie, what
happened to Dr. Wyman?

1406
00:53:50,533 --> 00:53:51,467
- He's dead.

1407
00:53:51,467 --> 00:53:52,833
- [Mike] Thank you, Julie.

1408
00:53:53,800 --> 00:53:54,933
- [Tom] Hey, wait up.

1409
00:53:54,933 --> 00:53:57,167
- Why're you staring at me?

1410
00:53:58,800 --> 00:54:00,667
You don't think that I had
anything to do with it?

1411
00:54:00,667 --> 00:54:01,467
- Johnny.

1412
00:54:01,467 --> 00:54:02,667
- [Mike] A.K.A. Steve.

1413
00:54:02,667 --> 00:54:03,467
- We're just as
confused as you are.

1414
00:54:03,467 --> 00:54:05,667
Did you see anything?

1415
00:54:05,667 --> 00:54:09,000
Was there anybody in the
room with you and Dr. Wyman?

1416
00:54:09,000 --> 00:54:10,233
- I don't know.

1417
00:54:10,233 --> 00:54:12,167
- I don't wanna hear
I don't know, mister.

1418
00:54:13,600 --> 00:54:16,033
- Last thing I remember--
♪ I was headed for the door ♪

1419
00:54:16,033 --> 00:54:17,533
- I was making my descent.

1420
00:54:17,533 --> 00:54:19,067
The jets didn't take hold.

1421
00:54:19,067 --> 00:54:20,967
When I finally released the
drag chute, it didn't hold.

1422
00:54:22,400 --> 00:54:23,967
I was too close to the ground.

1423
00:54:23,967 --> 00:54:25,400
- You crashed.

1424
00:54:25,400 --> 00:54:27,200
We brought you here.

1425
00:54:27,200 --> 00:54:28,200
- You're so full of it.

1426
00:54:28,200 --> 00:54:30,433
- You don't remember?

1427
00:54:30,433 --> 00:54:31,733
- I seemed to be floating,

1428
00:54:33,600 --> 00:54:36,467
in a warm, black mist.

1429
00:54:36,467 --> 00:54:38,867
- [Tom] Ah, we gave
you a pudding bath.

1430
00:54:38,867 --> 00:54:40,300
- Ohh, my head!

1431
00:54:40,300 --> 00:54:41,567
- [Mike] My achey, breaky head.

1432
00:54:41,567 --> 00:54:44,067
- My neck!
- [Crow] Have you seen it?

1433
00:54:44,067 --> 00:54:45,900
- Oh no, look at that.

1434
00:54:46,900 --> 00:54:48,167
- The same mark.

1435
00:54:48,167 --> 00:54:49,667
- [Mike] I told him
not to pick at that.

1436
00:54:51,000 --> 00:54:52,667
- [Tom] Oh, did that thing pop?

1437
00:54:52,667 --> 00:54:55,933
- Heuh huh huh, hey,
it's old pizza back.

1438
00:54:57,700 --> 00:55:00,433
- [Mike] See, I got a little
nutty with a wood chisel.

1439
00:55:01,400 --> 00:55:02,767
- What's this?

1440
00:55:02,767 --> 00:55:04,700
- [Tom] You think
I'm Steve, don't you?

1441
00:55:06,933 --> 00:55:08,367
- Tell me what's happening.

1442
00:55:08,367 --> 00:55:11,533
- Johnny, you don't understand.

1443
00:55:12,833 --> 00:55:14,300
We thought you were dead.

1444
00:55:14,300 --> 00:55:16,667
- And frankly, we're getting
pretty used to the idea.

1445
00:55:17,800 --> 00:55:19,233
- [Mike] Yeah, we
sold off your organs.

1446
00:55:19,233 --> 00:55:20,300
- Whaddya mean, dead?

1447
00:55:20,300 --> 00:55:22,100
- [Tom] Don't yell at us.

1448
00:55:22,100 --> 00:55:23,900
- When we pulled you
out of the compartment,

1449
00:55:23,900 --> 00:55:25,967
your body had completely
ceased to function.

1450
00:55:25,967 --> 00:55:27,400
- Whaddya mean, compartment?

1451
00:55:31,567 --> 00:55:34,200
- A hypometabolistic state?
- [Tom] No.

1452
00:55:34,200 --> 00:55:35,433
- [Mike] No.

1453
00:55:35,433 --> 00:55:36,700
- [John] Some type of
suspended animation?

1454
00:55:36,700 --> 00:55:37,933
- [Mike] No, no no
no, you were dead.

1455
00:55:37,933 --> 00:55:39,067
- [John] Brought on
by the contraction of

1456
00:55:39,067 --> 00:55:40,800
the mesenteric blood vessels?

1457
00:55:40,800 --> 00:55:42,167
- [Tom] That's so sad.

1458
00:55:43,400 --> 00:55:44,633
- That kind of terminology I'd

1459
00:55:44,633 --> 00:55:45,967
have expected from Dr.
Wyman, not from you.

1460
00:55:45,967 --> 00:55:47,900
- You wash your
mouth out with soap.

1461
00:55:47,900 --> 00:55:50,300
- Well, it seemed like
the natural diagnosis.

1462
00:55:50,300 --> 00:55:51,533
- From Dr. Wyman, yes.

1463
00:55:52,967 --> 00:55:56,733
- In some way I think Dr.
Wyman is influencing me.

1464
00:55:56,733 --> 00:55:58,267
I think he may be
a part of me now.

1465
00:55:58,267 --> 00:55:59,800
- [Mike] Can you be dead again?

1466
00:56:02,300 --> 00:56:04,467
- [Tom] I don't really
think that, ha ha ha.

1467
00:56:04,467 --> 00:56:06,567
- I don't understand!

1468
00:56:06,567 --> 00:56:08,600
You've got to help me.

1469
00:56:08,600 --> 00:56:09,433
Help me!

1470
00:56:11,067 --> 00:56:12,300
- This is unbelievable.

1471
00:56:12,300 --> 00:56:13,267
Things like this
just don't happen.

1472
00:56:13,267 --> 00:56:15,333
- [Crow] This is helping.

1473
00:56:18,000 --> 00:56:21,100
- [Tom] Thank you,
really, thank you.

1474
00:56:23,533 --> 00:56:24,600
- I'm not afraid.

1475
00:56:25,900 --> 00:56:28,033
I'm not afraid.
- [Mike] Afraid not am I.

1476
00:56:28,033 --> 00:56:30,300
- It didn't come
here to destroy.

1477
00:56:30,300 --> 00:56:33,400
- Johnny, try to tell us.

1478
00:56:33,400 --> 00:56:35,267
What didn't come
here to destroy us?

1479
00:56:35,267 --> 00:56:36,967
- No more questions,
more boobies.

1480
00:56:38,567 --> 00:56:40,067
- Is he all right?

1481
00:56:43,733 --> 00:56:45,700
- [Crow] Oh, this
one's not as nice.

1482
00:56:48,800 --> 00:56:49,733
- They're gone.

1483
00:56:49,733 --> 00:56:51,067
- [Crow] My bronies are gone.

1484
00:56:53,233 --> 00:56:55,533
- [Tom] I've got a
bassoon if we need one.

1485
00:56:59,633 --> 00:57:01,267
- What are you talking about?

1486
00:57:01,267 --> 00:57:02,467
- Come see for yourself.

1487
00:57:02,467 --> 00:57:04,967
- [Mike] Ya big, dumb,
dead guy, siddown.

1488
00:57:07,067 --> 00:57:09,433
(riffers vocalizing)

1489
00:57:09,433 --> 00:57:11,800
- Normal in every respect.

1490
00:57:11,800 --> 00:57:13,500
Four hours ago, it was populated

1491
00:57:13,500 --> 00:57:15,667
with alien, amorphic
cell structures.

1492
00:57:15,667 --> 00:57:17,700
- [Mike] They mean
to win Wimbledon.

1493
00:57:19,667 --> 00:57:21,000
- I think we should
see what I look

1494
00:57:21,000 --> 00:57:22,267
like under the fluoroscope.

1495
00:57:22,267 --> 00:57:24,100
- [Tom] Yeah, that'll
kill some time.

1496
00:57:25,533 --> 00:57:27,133
- [Mike] Put a shirt
on while you're at it.

1497
00:57:27,133 --> 00:57:30,200
- How will it work
with the power out?

1498
00:57:30,200 --> 00:57:32,133
- It operates on a
radium cathode tube.

1499
00:57:32,133 --> 00:57:32,967
- [Mike] Ohh.

1500
00:57:35,167 --> 00:57:37,433
- Wow, he's loaded
with shrimp, cool.

1501
00:57:39,367 --> 00:57:41,967
- The amorphic cell
structures have expanded to--

1502
00:57:41,967 --> 00:57:43,267
- [Mike] Jacksonville!

1503
